# Under Cover - a market pitch, a neighbour who undercuts, and what the trade asks of you.
# Written for the library, which is usually one person alone with a job. This one has a crowd in it.

title: Under Cover
start: start

== start ==
bg dining
music room_tone
Twelve pitches under cover and eleven of them have been the same eleven people for longer than
anyone can remember: the fruit, the bread, the man with the tools, the woman with the plants,
and you, with the cards and the china, since 2009.
The rain comes in sideways between the second and third pillar and always has. Everybody knows
which pitch is dry.
-> the_thursday

== the_thursday ==
On the Thursday there is a van in the third pitch with the back doors open and a man of about
thirty taking out folding tables, and by eleven he has a table of cards and china and a hand-
lettered board that says EVERYTHING A POUND.
He is two pitches down from you, which is not next to you, and he is selling what you sell.
-> the_day

== the_day ==
You take eleven pounds between you and the man takes about ninety, and the woman with the
plants, who you have known since 2009, comes over at two with her arms folded and says the
thing that everybody has been walking past your pitch not to say.
plants: He's got a box of it in the van and he's buying it off the internet.
you: I know.
plants: What are you going to do?
-> the_question

== the_question ==
That is the question, and it has four answers and you have heard three of them already today
from people who have never had a table here.
You have eleven pounds, a stock of cards that came from two house clearances, and a spot
between the second and third pillar out of the rain.
* Drop your prices. Everything a pound is not the floor. -> drop_prices
* Go and talk to him. Two minutes, properly. -> talk_to_him
* Carry on as you are and let the market decide. -> carry_on
* Tell the manager, who has a rule about it in the office. -> tell_manager

== drop_prices ==
You make a board of your own with a marker on the back of a box: EVERYTHING A POUND AND A HALF.
It takes him forty minutes to notice and twenty to answer with EVERYTHING A POUND FOR THE REST
OF THE DAY, and you can feel the eleven other pitches taking sides without saying anything, and
at four o'clock the whole row has a board out and only two of the eleven boards belong to people
who were selling cards this morning.
-> ending_race

== talk_to_him ==
You go over at half past two with two coffees, which costs you three pounds and is the most
expensive thing you have done all day.
He is friendly. He is genuinely friendly, and he tells you where he buys the boxes and what he
pays for them and it is less than a third of what you paid, and when you say that, he nods, and
says the thing that stops you: that he is not selling cards, he is selling a price, and that
prices are a thing you can buy by the crate.
-> the_after

== the_after ==
you: So what happens when everybody's table looks like yours.
man: It doesn't matter what the tables look like, mate, it matters which one's still here in
five years.
He says it without any edge at all, and he means it, and he is probably right, and it is the
end of a conversation that you had brought coffee to.
He is here the following Thursday and the one after that, and he is still friendly.
-> END

== carry_on ==
You do nothing. You take eleven pounds and then nine and then fourteen, and the man takes
ninety and then seventy and then about the same, and the market is exactly as loud on the third
Thursday as it was on the first.
The others ask you about it for a fortnight and then they stop asking, and the man's board
changes from EVERYTHING A POUND to EVERYTHING A POUND OR TWO, and nobody says anything about
that either, and the row carries on the way a row does.
-> END

== tell_manager ==
The manager is in the office by the gate and has been for twenty years and knows the rule, which
is in the licence and is about the same class of goods on adjacent pitches.
They come out and look at the two tables at eleven o'clock, side by side, yours with the cards
from two house clearances and his with the boxes from the internet, and they stand there for a
while with their arms folded.
manager: Adjacent's the word, though. You're two along.
you: I know.
manager: And I can't run a market on what a thing's worth.
-> ending_the_rule

== ending_race ==
The boards came down by the following Tuesday and by the Thursday after that the man had gone,
which is what a race to the bottom does to the person who can afford it least, and it was not
him.
The row is still eleven people. The woman with the plants still stands at two o'clock with her
arms folded, and she has never once mentioned the fortnight with the boards, and neither have
you, and it sits in the row like the rain coming in between the second and third pillar.
-> END

== ending_the_rule ==
Nothing happened. That is the whole of it: the manager looked at the two tables and said the
word adjacent and went back to the office, and the man kept coming on Thursdays and kept being
friendly and kept taking ninety pounds, and in the November he was two pitches down and in the
February he was in the end pitch where the rain comes in.
You are still here. You are still here in five years, which is what he said it was about, and
you take eleven pounds and nine pounds and fourteen pounds and you are here.
-> END
